import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import Conformance from '@/components/Conformance.astro'
import SignalFlow from '@/components/SignalFlow.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'
---

<Doc
  title="Integrating"
  description="Three ways to use a running mcpd: the HTTP API on its own, one of the six SDKs, or as an MCP server an agent can call. What each one costs you and what it gives back."
  lede="A daemon is running somewhere. Here are the three ways anything else reaches it, and how to choose between them."
  og="docs"
>
  <p>
    mcpd has one door and three ways through it. Everything below talks to the
    same HTTP surface: the SDKs are a convenience over it, and the MCP server is
    a client of it.
  </p>

  <h2>One port, four surfaces</h2>
  <p>
    A single process serves all of it on one port, 4317 by default. There is no
    sidecar, no collector to run beside it and no static-file server for the
    interface.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Path</th><th>What it is</th><th>Key</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/v1/traces</code>, <code>/v1/logs</code>, <code>/v1/metrics</code></td>
        <td>OpenTelemetry ingest. Any OTel SDK, with no mcpd-specific code in your application.</td>
        <td>Ingest</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/v1/ingest</code></td>
        <td>Native ingest. One batch carries any mix of the six signals and the whole data model, and one request carries a stream of batches.</td>
        <td>Ingest</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/v1/api/…</code></td>
        <td>The query API. The interface and the SDKs both read through this and nothing else.</td>
        <td>Read, write, admin, execute or worker, declared per endpoint</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/</code></td>
        <td>The operator interface, compiled into the binary.</td>
        <td>None for the markup; its data goes through the query API</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Two more paths, <code>/healthz</code> and <code>/readyz</code>, are
    unauthenticated so a probe can reach them. Start there when you are working
    out whether an endpoint is reachable at all:
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="is it there"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/healthz"' },
      { out: '{"status":"ok","version":"0.1.0","spec_version":"1.0.0",…}', tone: 'ok' },
      { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/readyz"' },
      { out: 'storage is not reachable: …', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '# readyz touches the store, so a 503 here means the daemon is up' },
      { out: '# and the database behind it is not.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The HTTP API, with no SDK</h2>
  <p>
    If your language has an HTTP client, it can talk to mcpd. There is nothing
    to register, no project to create up front and no handshake. Every SDK is a
    convenience over the requests below, and choosing this route costs you
    batching, retry and id generation, which you then do yourself.
  </p>

  <h3>Writing a run</h3>
  <p>
    <code>POST /v1/ingest</code> takes one batch. A batch may carry spans, logs,
    metrics, scores, feedback and aspect notes together, which is the reason the
    native door exists alongside the OTLP one: the six signals share trace and
    span ids, so sending them in one request keeps them correlated with no
    second lookup.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three shapes reach that endpoint, and the content type says which. Nothing
    is worked out from the bytes, so adding a newline inside a string can never
    change what a body means.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Content type</th><th>Body</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>application/json</code></td>
          <td>One batch object, which is what every SDK sends</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>application/json</code></td>
          <td>An array of batch objects, for a sender with several to hand</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>application/x-ndjson</code></td>
          <td>One batch per line, read as the body arrives rather than held whole. <code>application/jsonl</code> means the same thing</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Each element is the batch it is: it is stored in its own transaction when it
    carries an <code>ingest_key</code>, so a resent stream is recognised line by
    line rather than as one lump, and elements with no key are stored together.
    The whole request is refused, with nothing stored, if any line cannot be
    read or if the request as a whole is over either bound. The reply is one
    <code>IngestResult</code> covering all of it.
  </p>

  <SignalFlow class="not-prose my-8" />

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['curl']} region="trace" title="one batch" />

  <p>Four things the request has to get right:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ids are OpenTelemetry-shaped.</strong> 32 lowercase hex characters
      for a trace, 16 for a span, never all zero. Anything else breaks
      correlation with every other tool you run.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>ingest_key</code> makes a retry safe.</strong> It is an
      idempotency key that is stable for a batch, so a resend is recognised and
      counted as a duplicate rather than written twice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The key goes in a header.</strong>
      <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>, or <code>X-Mcpd-Key</code>. Ingest
      needs an ingest key, and only when the daemon has any configured.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Batches have bounds.</strong> 32 MiB of body and 10,000 signals by
      default, both settable with <code>--max-body-bytes</code> and
      <code>--max-batch-signals</code>. The signal bound counts the whole
      request, so splitting the same signals over lines does not get past it.
      Send <code>content-encoding: gzip</code> for anything large.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The response reports what happened per signal:
    <code>{'{"accepted":2,"rejected":0,"duplicate":0}'}</code>, with an
    <code>errors</code> array naming the index and the reason for anything
    refused. The OTLP endpoints answer in OTLP's own shape instead, with a
    <code>partialSuccess</code> object carrying the rejected count, because that
    is what an OpenTelemetry sender reads.
  </p>

  <h3>Reading it back</h3>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['curl']} region="query" title="read" />

  <p>
    Filters compose, paging is by cursor, and <code>project</code> defaults to
    the daemon's default project when you leave it out. Full-text search over
    stored payloads is the <code>q</code> parameter, which is what makes
    "find the run where the model said this" a query rather than a grep.
  </p>
  <p>
    A failure mcpd itself raises comes back as JSON with a stable
    machine-readable <code>error</code> code, a <code>message</code> written for
    whoever has to fix it, and a <code>field</code> when one parameter is at
    fault. Branch on the code, show the message.
  </p>
  <p>
    One class of failure does not have that shape, and a client that assumes it
    does will fail to parse the reply. A request body that cannot be decoded
    into the type the endpoint expects is refused by the web framework before
    any mcpd code runs, so it answers <code>422</code> with
    <code>content-type: text/plain</code> and a message like
    <code>missing field `scorer_id`</code>. Body syntax that is not JSON at all
    answers <code>400</code>, also as text. There is no <code>error</code> code
    to branch on in either case. This covers every endpoint that takes a typed
    body:
    <code>POST /v1/api/scores</code>, <code>/v1/api/feedback</code>,
    <code>/v1/api/score</code>, <code>/v1/api/datasets</code>, its
    <code>items</code> and <code>capture</code> routes, and
    <code>/v1/api/experiments</code>. The ingest doors read the body themselves,
    so <code>/v1/ingest</code> and the OTLP routes do return the JSON shape.
    Treat a non-JSON body on a 4xx as a malformed request from your own code,
    and read it as text.
  </p>

  <h2>The SDKs</h2>
  <p>
    Six languages: <a href="/docs/sdks/typescript" class="link">TypeScript</a>,
    <a href="/docs/sdks/python" class="link">Python</a>,
    <a href="/docs/sdks/rust" class="link">Rust</a>,
    <a href="/docs/sdks/go" class="link">Go</a>,
    <a href="/docs/sdks/kotlin" class="link">Kotlin</a> and
    <a href="/docs/sdks/elixir" class="link">Elixir</a>. Each is idiomatic where
    it can be and identical where it matters. A user who has read the TypeScript
    quickstart can predict the Kotlin one.
  </p>
  <p>What you get over raw HTTP:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Batching and a background worker, so recording a span appends to a queue and returns.</li>
    <li>Retry with backoff on 429 and the 5xx codes worth retrying, and never on a 4xx that will stay malformed.</li>
    <li>Id generation, context propagation, and W3C <code>traceparent</code> parsed on the way in and emitted on the way out, so a trace survives a service boundary.</li>
    <li>Types generated from the same specification as the daemon's, so a field cannot mean one thing in the SDK and another in the store.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Three promises hold in every language, and they are the reason an SDK is
    safe to put in a hot path. Instrumentation never throws: an unreachable
    daemon is logged at most once an interval and swallowed. It never blocks:
    network work happens on the worker, so your agent's latency does not depend
    on mcpd being up. And its queue is bounded: when it fills, the oldest spans
    are dropped and a counter goes up, because an unbounded buffer turns a
    network outage into an out-of-memory kill.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" region="init" title="connect" />

  <p>
    A client built with no arguments works against a local daemon. Everything is
    settable in code or by environment variable, with code winning, and the
    variable names are the same in every language so a value copied between two
    services keeps working when they are written in different ones:
    <code>MCPD_ENDPOINT</code>, <code>MCPD_API_KEY</code>,
    <code>MCPD_PROJECT</code>, <code>MCPD_ENVIRONMENT</code>,
    <code>MCPD_SERVICE_NAME</code>, <code>MCPD_SERVICE_VERSION</code>,
    <code>MCPD_SAMPLE</code> and <code>MCPD_ENABLED</code>. Setting
    <code>MCPD_ENABLED=false</code> turns every call into a no-op that still
    returns valid objects, so a test suite can switch mcpd off without touching
    any code.
  </p>

  <h3>Recording a model call</h3>
  <p>
    Hand a provider's response to the span and the SDK reads the rest:
    <code>recordResponse</code> in TypeScript and Kotlin,
    <code>record_response</code> in Python and Rust,
    <code>RecordResponse</code> in Go, and <code>Mcpd.ModelCall.options/2</code>
    in Elixir. It takes an OpenAI chat-completions or responses-API response,
    or an Anthropic messages response, and records the model, the provider,
    the token usage with its cache and reasoning meters, and the response id,
    finish reasons and service tier. It never records the text, which stays
    yours to keep or not. <code>usage_from_openai</code> and
    <code>usage_from_anthropic</code> do the token mapping alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mark_first_token</code> records the time to the first streamed token
    as <code>mcpd.time_to_first_token_ms</code>, once, so it can sit inside a
    streaming loop. <code>set_guardrail</code> records whether a guardrail
    tripped, and why.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <code>redact</code> client option is a function applied to every span's
    input, output, attributes and metadata, and every log line's fields, before
    anything is queued, so a value you mask never leaves the process. A redactor
    that throws drops the field rather than sending the original, and the
    client's stats count it.
  </p>

  <h3>They are interchangeable, and that is tested</h3>
  <p>
    Unit tests prove each SDK does what its own authors meant. They prove
    nothing about whether the six agree. So the conformance suite writes one
    canonical run through each language against one live daemon, plus a seventh
    channel that sends the same run with nothing but <code>curl</code>, and
    asserts that what comes back is structurally identical:
  </p>

  <Conformance class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    If you are choosing a language for a new service, this is the answer to
    "will the traces look the same as the ones we already have".
  </p>

  <h3>If your language is not one of the six</h3>
  <p>
    Point an OpenTelemetry SDK at <code>/v1/traces</code> and send no new code.
    mcpd is an OTLP receiver, and it maps both the OpenTelemetry
    <code>gen_ai.*</code> convention and the OpenInference
    <code>llm.*</code> one, so instrumentation you already have lands correctly.
    See <a href="/docs/opentelemetry" class="link">OpenTelemetry</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>From an AI agent, over MCP</h2>
  <p>
    The third route is for an agent rather than a service: instead of your code
    reading traces, a coding or support agent reads them on your behalf and
    answers a question about them. "Why did the last run fail", "show me the
    runs a scorer marked down this week", "what did this session cost". mcpd
    exposes that through the Model Context Protocol.
  </p>

  <h3>The three tools</h3>
  <p>
    Three, not thirty. They are the three moves of one investigation: which run,
    what happened inside it, and whether it is one run or all of them.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it answers</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>find_runs</code></td>
        <td>
          Which run. One line each: id, age, status, duration, cost, steps.
          Filter by status, model, user, session or free text; sort by duration
          to get the slowest. Defaults to the last 24 hours.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>get_run</code></td>
        <td>
          What happened. The waterfall, the step that failed and why, the prompts
          and completions, the logs, the scores. Payloads are capped so one run
          cannot fill a context window.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>run_stats</code></td>
        <td>
          One run or all of them. Cost, failure rate, latency and tokens over a
          window, grouped by model, name, status, environment or user. A 40%
          failure rate means the run you are reading is not the problem.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Results come back as text rather than JSON, which is a deliberate choice
    about whose context this spends. Forty span objects is mostly punctuation;
    the same run drawn as a waterfall is a few hundred tokens and the agent can
    see the shape of it.
  </p>

  <h3>The shape of it</h3>
  <p>
    The MCP server is a subcommand of the same binary rather than a second
    service. It runs as <code>mcpd mcp</code>, speaks JSON-RPC over standard
    input and output, and holds no data of its own. There is no port to open, no
    process to keep alive and nothing to deploy: the agent host starts it as a
    child process when a conversation needs it and stops it afterwards.
  </p>
  <p>
    What it does hold is a connection to a daemon. Every tool call becomes a
    request to the same query API described above, with the same read key and
    the same project scoping, so an agent cannot reach a project the key it was
    given does not reach. The blast radius of handing an agent this server is
    exactly the blast radius of handing it that key, which is the property to
    check before you configure one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tool set is deliberately small. A large surface makes a model choose
    badly, and most questions about an agent's behaviour reduce to listing runs,
    opening one, and asking a numeric question over a window. A client discovers
    the current set with the protocol's own <code>tools/list</code> call rather
    than from a list written down here that could fall behind.
  </p>

  <h3>Configuring a client</h3>
  <p>
    An MCP host launches a server from a command, some arguments and an
    environment. The exact file and the exact key are the host's, not mcpd's, so
    check its documentation; the three values it needs from you are the same
    everywhere.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What the host needs</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Command</td><td>The <code>mcpd</code> binary, on a path the host can run.</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Arguments</td>
        <td>
          The <code>mcp</code> subcommand. It takes <code>--endpoint</code>, the
          daemon to query, and <code>--api-key</code>, a read key for it, for a
          host that passes arguments more readily than an environment.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Environment</td>
        <td>
          The same two values as <code>MCPD_ENDPOINT</code> and
          <code>MCPD_API_KEY</code>. The endpoint defaults to
          <code>http://127.0.0.1:4317</code>, and the key can be left out when
          the daemon has no keys set.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    label="what the host runs"
    command="MCPD_ENDPOINT=https://mcpd.internal MCPD_API_KEY=$READ mcpd mcp"
    primaryLabel="As environment"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'As flags', command: 'mcpd mcp --endpoint https://mcpd.internal --api-key $READ' },
      { label: 'From Docker', command: 'docker run -i --rm -e MCPD_ENDPOINT -e MCPD_API_KEY ghcr.io/mcpd/mcpd mcp' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>Three things to hold on to when you issue that key.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Give the agent a <code>read</code> key, not an <code>ingest</code> key.
      The scopes exist so that a credential for writing spans is not also a
      credential for every stored prompt.
    </li>
    <li>
      Scope it to a project. A key configured at the top level reaches every
      project on the daemon.
    </li>
    <li>
      Know that a read key also deletes traces until you configure an
      <code>admin</code> list, because the admin scope falls back to the read
      list when it is unset. An agent holding a read key on a daemon that has
      never been given an admin list can delete a run.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>All three are on <a href="/docs/security" class="link">Security</a>.</p>

  <h2>Which one to use</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Route</th><th>Use it when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>HTTP</td>
        <td>Your language has no SDK, you are sending from a shell or a job, or you want no dependency at all.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>An SDK</td>
        <td>You are instrumenting an application, where batching, retry and never throwing are worth more than the dependency.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>OpenTelemetry</td>
        <td>You already emit OTel and would rather change a URL than add a library.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>MCP</td>
        <td>A person is asking an agent questions about stored runs, rather than code reading them on a schedule.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    They mix. A fleet that emits through the TypeScript SDK, a nightly job that
    posts scores with <code>curl</code>, and an agent reading it all back over
    MCP is one deployment, not three.
  </p>

